en:

'<p class="l-text"><strong>Statistics and sorting are the backbone of exploratory data analysis.</strong> Before you feed data into any machine learning model, you need to understand its distribution, spot correlations between features, find outliers, and organize values. NumPy provides a complete toolkit for all of these tasks — fast, vectorized, and ready for arrays of any size.</p>'

+ '<p class="l-text">This lesson covers six core areas: descriptive statistics, correlation and covariance, sorting and searching, set operations, histogram and binning, and basic statistical testing. Each section includes real ML connections so you can see exactly when and why these tools matter.</p>'
+ '<div class="lesson-outcomes" style="background:rgba(200,169,110,0.06);border-left:3px solid #c8a96e;padding:1rem 1.2rem;margin:1.5rem 0;border-radius:0 8px 8px 0"><div style="font-size:0.78rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:0.08em;color:#c8a96e;margin-bottom:0.6rem">📍 WHAT YOU\'LL LEARN</div><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.3rem;line-height:1.65;font-size:0.93rem;color:rgba(235,230,220,0.92)"><li>Compute mean, median, variance, std, percentiles, and correlations on arrays</li><li>Read a histogram and compute frequencies with np.histogram and bincount</li><li>Detect outliers using IQR and z-score methods on real data</li><li>Aggregate along an axis to summarize rows or columns of a matrix</li><li>Distinguish population vs sample statistics and pick the correct ddof</li></ul></div>'


/* ============================================================
   SECTION 1: Descriptive Statistics
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">1. Descriptive Statistics</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>The first step of any data project:</strong> Before building models, compute descriptive statistics. Mean, median, standard deviation, and percentiles give you an instant snapshot of your data — its center, spread, and shape. NumPy computes all of these in microseconds, even on millions of data points.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Mean (arithmetic average) — the center of mass of your data:</strong></p>'
+ '<div class="katex-block">$$\\mu = \\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N} x_i$$</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">μ</div><div class="card-body">Population mean — the average value. Sensitive to outliers: a single extreme value can shift it dramatically.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">N</div><div class="card-body">Sample size — total number of observations in the dataset.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">x_i</div><div class="card-body">The i-th observation. The sum runs from i=1 to N, covering every data point exactly once.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Σ</div><div class="card-body">Summation operator — adds all values together. Divided by N gives the average.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Variance — how spread out the data is around the mean:</strong></p>'
+ '<div class="katex-block">$$\\sigma^2 = \\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}(x_i - \\mu)^2$$</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">σ²</div><div class="card-body">Variance — average squared deviation from the mean. Always non-negative. Units are squared (if x is in meters, σ² is in m²).</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">(x_i - μ)</div><div class="card-body">Deviation of one observation from the mean. Can be positive or negative.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">(x_i - μ)²</div><div class="card-body">Squared deviation — eliminates sign cancellation, penalizes large deviations more than small ones.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">1/N vs 1/(N-1)</div><div class="card-body">np.var uses 1/N by default (population). For sample variance pass ddof=1 to divide by N-1 (Bessel correction).</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Standard deviation — variance back in the original units:</strong></p>'
+ '<div class="katex-block">$$\\sigma = \\sqrt{\\sigma^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}(x_i - \\mu)^2}$$</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">σ</div><div class="card-body">Standard deviation — square root of variance. Same units as the data, easy to interpret directly.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">68-95-99.7 rule</div><div class="card-body">For normal data: ~68% within μ±σ, ~95% within μ±2σ, ~99.7% within μ±3σ. Foundation of confidence intervals.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">σ vs σ²</div><div class="card-body">Std (σ) is preferred for reporting because units match data. Variance (σ²) is preferred in math because it is additive for independent variables.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>IQR (Interquartile Range) — robust spread measure:</strong></p>'
+ '<div class="katex-block">$$\\text{IQR} = Q_3 - Q_1$$</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Q1</div><div class="card-body">First quartile (25th percentile) — 25% of data falls below this value.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Q3</div><div class="card-body">Third quartile (75th percentile) — 75% of data falls below this value.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">IQR</div><div class="card-body">The middle 50% of the data. Unlike std, IQR is not affected by extreme outliers, making it the robust alternative for skewed data.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">1.5×IQR rule</div><div class="card-body">Outlier detection: values below Q1 - 1.5·IQR or above Q3 + 1.5·IQR are typically flagged as outliers.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Central tendency</strong> measures where the "middle" of your data is:</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Mean</div><div class="card-body">np.mean(x) — arithmetic average. Sensitive to outliers. The most common measure of center.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Median</div><div class="card-body">np.median(x) — middle value when sorted. Robust to outliers. Better for skewed distributions.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Std Dev</div><div class="card-body">np.std(x) — measures spread around the mean. Low = tightly clustered, high = widely spread.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Variance</div><div class="card-body">np.var(x) — std squared. Used in math formulas (e.g., Gaussian PDF). Units are squared.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Percentile</div><div class="card-body">np.percentile(x, q) — value below which q% of data falls. P50 = median, P25/P75 = quartiles.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Quantile</div><div class="card-body">np.quantile(x, q) — same as percentile but q is 0\u20131 instead of 0\u2013100. Q(0.5) = median.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># Generate sample data: exam scores</span>\nnp.random.<span class="fn">seed</span>(<span class="num">42</span>)\nscores = np.random.<span class="fn">normal</span>(loc=<span class="num">72</span>, scale=<span class="num">12</span>, size=<span class="num">1000</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Central tendency</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Mean:  "</span>, np.<span class="fn">mean</span>(scores))      <span class="cm"># ~72.0</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Median:"</span>, np.<span class="fn">median</span>(scores))    <span class="cm"># ~72.0</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Spread</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Std:   "</span>, np.<span class="fn">std</span>(scores))       <span class="cm"># ~12.0</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Var:   "</span>, np.<span class="fn">var</span>(scores))       <span class="cm"># ~144.0</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Range</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Min:   "</span>, np.<span class="fn">min</span>(scores))       <span class="cm"># ~35</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Max:   "</span>, np.<span class="fn">max</span>(scores))       <span class="cm"># ~108</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Range: "</span>, np.<span class="fn">ptp</span>(scores))       <span class="cm"># max - min</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Percentiles and quartiles</span>\nq1, q2, q3 = np.<span class="fn">percentile</span>(scores, [<span class="num">25</span>, <span class="num">50</span>, <span class="num">75</span>])\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Q1={q1:.1f}, Median={q2:.1f}, Q3={q3:.1f}"</span>)\niqr = q3 - q1\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"IQR={iqr:.1f}"</span>)  <span class="cm"># interquartile range</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Detect outliers using IQR method</span>\nlower = q1 - <span class="num">1.5</span> * iqr\nupper = q3 + <span class="num">1.5</span> * iqr\noutliers = scores[(scores < lower) | (scores > upper)]\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Outliers: {len(outliers)} values"</span>)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>ddof parameter:</strong> By default, <code>np.std()</code> and <code>np.var()</code> compute the <strong>population</strong> statistic (dividing by N). For <strong>sample</strong> statistics (dividing by N-1), pass <code>ddof=1</code>. In ML, population statistics are typically used since we operate on entire datasets, but in classical statistics you almost always want <code>ddof=1</code>.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Axis parameter</strong> — computing statistics along specific dimensions is critical for ML. When you have a dataset as a 2D array (rows = samples, columns = features), you often need per-feature statistics:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># Dataset: 5 samples, 3 features</span>\ndata = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="num">170</span>, <span class="num">65</span>, <span class="num">25</span>],   <span class="cm"># height, weight, age</span>\n                [<span class="num">180</span>, <span class="num">80</span>, <span class="num">30</span>],\n                [<span class="num">165</span>, <span class="num">55</span>, <span class="num">22</span>],\n                [<span class="num">175</span>, <span class="num">70</span>, <span class="num">28</span>],\n                [<span class="num">190</span>, <span class="num">90</span>, <span class="num">35</span>]])\n\n<span class="cm"># axis=0: aggregate ACROSS samples (column-wise) \u2192 one value per feature</span>\nfeature_means = np.<span class="fn">mean</span>(data, axis=<span class="num">0</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Feature means:"</span>, feature_means)  <span class="cm"># [176.  72.  28.]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># axis=1: aggregate ACROSS features (row-wise) \u2192 one value per sample</span>\nsample_means = np.<span class="fn">mean</span>(data, axis=<span class="num">1</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Sample means:"</span>, sample_means)    <span class="cm"># [86.67 96.67 80.67 91.0 105.0]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Feature normalization (z-score): (x - mean) / std</span>\nmeans = np.<span class="fn">mean</span>(data, axis=<span class="num">0</span>)\nstds  = np.<span class="fn">std</span>(data, axis=<span class="num">0</span>)\nnormalized = (data - means) / stds   <span class="cm"># broadcasting!</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Normalized means:"</span>, np.<span class="fn">mean</span>(normalized, axis=<span class="num">0</span>))   <span class="cm"># ~[0, 0, 0]</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Normalized stds: "</span>, np.<span class="fn">std</span>(normalized, axis=<span class="num">0</span>))    <span class="cm"># [1, 1, 1]</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="think-box"><div class="think-label">THINK ABOUT IT</div><div class="think-body">Feature normalization (z-score scaling) is one of the most common preprocessing steps in ML. Without it, features with large magnitudes (like height in cm) dominate the loss function over features with small magnitudes (like age). <code>(x - mean) / std</code> centers each feature at 0 with unit variance, giving all features equal footing. scikit-learn\'s <code>StandardScaler</code> does exactly this under the hood.</div></div>'

