/* ============================================================
   SECTION 2: Correlation & Covariance
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">2. Correlation & Covariance</h2>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Correlation and covariance</strong> measure how two variables move together. In ML, they are crucial for feature selection (drop highly correlated features), understanding relationships between variables, and detecting multicollinearity in linear models.</p>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Covariance — joint variability of two variables:</strong></p>'
+ '<div class="katex-block">$$\\text{Cov}(X, Y) = \\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}(x_i - \\mu_X)(y_i - \\mu_Y)$$</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Cov(X,Y)</div><div class="card-body">Covariance — measures how X and Y vary together. Positive: they move in the same direction. Negative: opposite directions. Zero: no linear association.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">μ_X, μ_Y</div><div class="card-body">Means of X and Y respectively. Each variable is centered before multiplication.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">(x_i-μ_X)(y_i-μ_Y)</div><div class="card-body">Product of paired deviations. When both deviations have the same sign, the product is positive — driving covariance up.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Scale-dependent</div><div class="card-body">Covariance magnitude depends on the units of X and Y. Hard to compare across variable pairs — that is why we normalize to correlation.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Pearson correlation — scale-free version of covariance:</strong></p>'
+ '<div class="katex-block">$$\\rho_{XY} = \\frac{\\text{Cov}(X, Y)}{\\sigma_X \\, \\sigma_Y}$$</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">ρ (rho)</div><div class="card-body">Pearson correlation coefficient — bounded between -1 and +1. Standard measure of linear association between two variables.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">σ_X · σ_Y</div><div class="card-body">Product of standard deviations — normalizes covariance to be unitless. This is why correlation is comparable across variable pairs.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">ρ = +1</div><div class="card-body">Perfect positive linear relationship. As X increases, Y increases proportionally on a straight line.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">ρ = -1</div><div class="card-body">Perfect negative linear relationship. As X increases, Y decreases proportionally on a straight line.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">ρ ≈ 0</div><div class="card-body">No LINEAR relationship. Beware: a strong nonlinear relationship (like Y = X²) can still give ρ ≈ 0!</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Covariance</div><div class="card-body">np.cov(x, y) — measures joint variability. Positive = move together, negative = move opposite. Magnitude depends on scale.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Correlation</div><div class="card-body">np.corrcoef(x, y) — normalized covariance, always between -1 and +1. Scale-independent. The standard measure of linear relationship.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">No Correlation</div><div class="card-body">r \u2248 0 means no LINEAR relationship (there could still be a nonlinear one). Don\'t confuse with independence!</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Perfect Correlation</div><div class="card-body">r = +1 means perfect positive linear relationship. r = -1 means perfect negative. Rarely seen in real data.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\nnp.random.<span class="fn">seed</span>(<span class="num">42</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Simulate correlated features</span>\nheight = np.random.<span class="fn">normal</span>(<span class="num">170</span>, <span class="num">10</span>, <span class="num">200</span>)       <span class="cm"># cm</span>\nweight = <span class="num">0.8</span> * height + np.random.<span class="fn">normal</span>(<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">8</span>, <span class="num">200</span>)  <span class="cm"># correlated with height</span>\nshoe_size = np.random.<span class="fn">normal</span>(<span class="num">42</span>, <span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">200</span>)     <span class="cm"># weakly correlated</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Correlation coefficient matrix</span>\ncorr = np.<span class="fn">corrcoef</span>([height, weight, shoe_size])\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Correlation matrix:"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(np.<span class="fn">round</span>(corr, <span class="num">3</span>))\n<span class="cm"># [[1.    0.78  0.03]    height-height, height-weight, height-shoe</span>\n<span class="cm">#  [0.78  1.    0.02]    weight-height, weight-weight, weight-shoe</span>\n<span class="cm">#  [0.03  0.02  1.   ]]  shoe-height,   shoe-weight,   shoe-shoe</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Covariance matrix</span>\ncov = np.<span class="fn">cov</span>([height, weight, shoe_size])\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"\\nCovariance matrix:"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(np.<span class="fn">round</span>(cov, <span class="num">1</span>))\n\n<span class="cm"># Extract pairwise correlation</span>\nr_hw = np.<span class="fn">corrcoef</span>(height, weight)[<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">1</span>]\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"\\nHeight-Weight correlation: {r_hw:.3f}"</span>)  <span class="cm"># ~0.78</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare">'
+ '<div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">COVARIANCE</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Magnitude depends on variable scales</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Units: product of variable units</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Range: (-\u221e, +\u221e)</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Hard to interpret across variables</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Used in PCA, Mahalanobis distance</div></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">CORRELATION</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Scale-independent (normalized)</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Unitless</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Range: [-1, +1]</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Easy to interpret: closer to \u00b11 = stronger</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item">Used in feature selection, EDA</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

