+ 'Plotly.newPlot("plot-corr-en",[t1,t3,t2],layout,{responsive:true,displayModeBar:false});'
+ '},200)</script>'

+ '<div class="calc-graph"><div class="graph-caption"><strong>What this graph shows:</strong> Left panel: height vs weight with a strong positive correlation (r \u2248 0.78) — points cluster tightly around the trend line. Right panel: height vs shoe size with no correlation (r \u2248 0.03) — points scattered randomly. In ML, strongly correlated features carry redundant information and one can often be dropped.</div></div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>Feature selection tip:</strong> When building linear models, check the correlation matrix first. If two features have |r| > 0.9, they are nearly collinear — consider dropping one. High multicollinearity inflates coefficient variance and makes the model unstable. Use <code>np.corrcoef(X.T)</code> where X has shape (n_samples, n_features).</div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 3: Sorting & Searching
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">3. Sorting & Searching</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Why sorting matters in ML:</strong> Finding top-k predictions, ranking features by importance, computing percentiles, building decision trees (which sort features at every split), and efficient nearest-neighbor search all depend on sorting. NumPy\'s sort functions run in O(n log n) compiled C, far faster than Python\'s sorted().</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text">NumPy provides several sorting and searching functions, each optimized for different use cases:</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">np.sort</div><div class="card-body">Returns a sorted copy of the array. Does not modify the original. O(n log n) using introsort by default.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">np.argsort</div><div class="card-body">Returns the indices that would sort the array. Essential for sorting one array by another (e.g., sort names by scores).</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">np.partition</div><div class="card-body">Partially sorts: k-th smallest element is in position k, smaller elements before, larger after. O(n) — much faster than full sort for top-k!</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">np.lexsort</div><div class="card-body">Sorts by multiple keys (like SQL ORDER BY col1, col2). Last key is primary sort key.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># Basic sorting</span>\narr = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">42</span>, <span class="num">15</span>, <span class="num">88</span>, <span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">67</span>, <span class="num">29</span>])\nsorted_arr = np.<span class="fn">sort</span>(arr)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Sorted:"</span>, sorted_arr)      <span class="cm"># [ 3 15 29 42 67 88]</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Original:"</span>, arr)            <span class="cm"># [42 15 88  3 67 29] — unchanged!</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># In-place sort (modifies the array)</span>\narr.<span class="fn">sort</span>()\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"After .sort():"</span>, arr)       <span class="cm"># [ 3 15 29 42 67 88]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Reverse sort (no built-in — use slicing trick)</span>\narr_desc = np.<span class="fn">sort</span>(arr)[::-<span class="num">1</span>]\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Descending:"</span>, arr_desc)     <span class="cm"># [88 67 42 29 15  3]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># argsort — returns indices that sort the array</span>\nscores = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">85</span>, <span class="num">92</span>, <span class="num">78</span>, <span class="num">95</span>, <span class="num">88</span>])\nnames = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="str">"Bob"</span>, <span class="str">"Carol"</span>, <span class="str">"Dave"</span>, <span class="str">"Eve"</span>])\norder = np.<span class="fn">argsort</span>(scores)[::-<span class="num">1</span>]   <span class="cm"># indices for descending scores</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Ranking:"</span>, names[order])    <span class="cm"># [Dave Bob Eve Alice Carol]</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Scores: "</span>, scores[order])   <span class="cm"># [95 92 88 85 78]</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-steps">'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">np.sort(a) vs a.sort()</div><div class="step-detail"><code>np.sort(a)</code> returns a new sorted array (original untouched). <code>a.sort()</code> sorts in-place (modifies the original, returns None). In ML pipelines, prefer <code>np.sort()</code> to avoid side effects.</div></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">2D Sorting with axis</div><div class="step-detail"><code>np.sort(matrix, axis=0)</code> sorts each column independently. <code>axis=1</code> sorts each row. <code>axis=-1</code> (default) sorts along the last axis. Use <code>axis=None</code> to flatten and sort all elements.</div></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Stable vs Unstable Sorts</div><div class="step-detail"><code>np.sort(a, kind="stable")</code> preserves the relative order of equal elements (mergesort). Default <code>kind="quicksort"</code> (actually introsort) is faster but unstable. Use stable when you need deterministic ordering of ties.</div></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-content"><div class="step-title">Top-k with np.partition</div><div class="step-detail"><code>np.partition(a, k)</code> runs in O(n) — much faster than O(n log n) full sort when you only need the k smallest/largest elements. For top-5 predictions out of 10,000 classes, this is 10x faster than full sort.</div></div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># --- Partition: O(n) top-k selection ---</span>\npredictions = np.random.<span class="fn">rand</span>(<span class="num">10000</span>)  <span class="cm"># model output probabilities</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Top-5 highest predictions (without full sort!)</span>\nk = <span class="num">5</span>\ntop_k_idx = np.<span class="fn">argpartition</span>(predictions, -k)[-k:]\ntop_k_idx = top_k_idx[np.<span class="fn">argsort</span>(predictions[top_k_idx])[::-<span class="num">1</span>]]\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Top-5 indices:"</span>, top_k_idx)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Top-5 values: "</span>, predictions[top_k_idx])\n\n<span class="cm"># --- lexsort: multi-key sorting ---</span>\n<span class="cm"># Sort students by grade (primary) then by name (secondary)</span>\ngrades = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">3</span>])\nnames_idx = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">4</span>])  <span class="cm"># Alice=0, Bob=1, ...</span>\norder = np.<span class="fn">lexsort</span>((names_idx, grades))  <span class="cm"># last key is primary!</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Sorted order:"</span>, order)  <span class="cm"># grades ascending, names as tiebreaker</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># --- searchsorted: binary search in sorted array ---</span>\nsorted_arr = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">10</span>, <span class="num">20</span>, <span class="num">30</span>, <span class="num">40</span>, <span class="num">50</span>])\nidx = np.<span class="fn">searchsorted</span>(sorted_arr, <span class="num">25</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Insert 25 at index:"</span>, idx)  <span class="cm"># 2 (between 20 and 30)</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Batch search: where would these values go?</span>\nnew_vals = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">5</span>, <span class="num">15</span>, <span class="num">35</span>, <span class="num">55</span>])\npositions = np.<span class="fn">searchsorted</span>(sorted_arr, new_vals)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Positions:"</span>, positions)  <span class="cm"># [0 1 3 5]</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-example"><div class="example-label">ML APPLICATION</div><div class="example-body"><strong>Top-k predictions in classification:</strong> When a model outputs probabilities for 10,000 classes (e.g., ImageNet), you rarely need all classes sorted. <code>np.argpartition(probs, -5)[-5:]</code> gives you the top-5 predictions in O(n) time instead of O(n log n). For real-time inference, this difference matters. The same trick works for finding the k-nearest neighbors in a distance matrix.</div></div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 4: Set Operations
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">4. Set Operations</h2>'

+ '<p class="l-text">NumPy provides fast set operations on arrays — finding unique values, intersections, unions, and differences. These are essential for data cleaning, vocabulary building in NLP, and comparing datasets.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">np.unique</div><div class="card-body">Returns sorted unique elements. With return_counts=True, also counts occurrences — the fastest value_counts for arrays.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">np.intersect1d</div><div class="card-body">Elements common to both arrays. Useful for finding shared vocabulary between two corpora.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">np.union1d</div><div class="card-body">All unique elements from both arrays combined. Building a merged vocabulary.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">np.setdiff1d</div><div class="card-body">Elements in array A that are NOT in array B. Finding words unique to one document.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># np.unique — find unique values and their counts</span>\nlabels = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">2</span>])\nunique_vals, counts = np.<span class="fn">unique</span>(labels, return_counts=<span class="kw">True</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Classes:"</span>, unique_vals)  <span class="cm"># [0 1 2]</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Counts: "</span>, counts)       <span class="cm"># [3 3 4]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># unique with extra outputs</span>\nuniq, idx_first, inverse, cnt = np.<span class="fn">unique</span>(labels,\n    return_index=<span class="kw">True</span>, return_inverse=<span class="kw">True</span>, return_counts=<span class="kw">True</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"First occurrence:"</span>, idx_first)  <span class="cm"># [1 2 0]</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Inverse mapping: "</span>, inverse)    <span class="cm"># reconstruct original: uniq[inverse]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Set operations</span>\ntrain_ids = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">5</span>, <span class="num">8</span>, <span class="num">13</span>])\ntest_ids  = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">3</span>, <span class="num">7</span>, <span class="num">11</span>, <span class="num">13</span>])\n\n<span class="cm"># Intersection: IDs in both sets (data leakage check!)</span>\nshared = np.<span class="fn">intersect1d</span>(train_ids, test_ids)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Shared IDs:"</span>, shared)  <span class="cm"># [ 2  3 13] \u2190 data leakage!</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Union: all unique IDs</span>\nall_ids = np.<span class="fn">union1d</span>(train_ids, test_ids)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"All IDs:   "</span>, all_ids)   <span class="cm"># [ 1  2  3  5  7  8 11 13]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Difference: IDs only in train (not in test)</span>\ntrain_only = np.<span class="fn">setdiff1d</span>(train_ids, test_ids)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Train only:"</span>, train_only) <span class="cm"># [1 5 8]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Membership test: which train_ids are in test_ids?</span>\nmask = np.<span class="fn">isin</span>(train_ids, test_ids)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"In test?:  "</span>, mask)  <span class="cm"># [False True True False False True]</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-example"><div class="example-label">NLP APPLICATION</div><div class="example-body"><strong>Vocabulary operations:</strong> In NLP, you often need to compare vocabularies across datasets. <code>np.intersect1d(vocab_train, vocab_test)</code> finds shared words. <code>np.setdiff1d(vocab_test, vocab_train)</code> finds out-of-vocabulary (OOV) words that the model has never seen — these are often replaced with an &lt;UNK&gt; token. <code>np.unique(tokens, return_counts=True)</code> gives you word frequencies instantly.</div></div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>np.isin vs np.in1d:</strong> <code>np.isin(a, b)</code> is the modern replacement for <code>np.in1d(a, b)</code>. Both return a boolean mask indicating which elements of <code>a</code> are found in <code>b</code>. Use <code>np.isin()</code> in new code — it preserves the shape of the input array, while <code>np.in1d()</code> always returns a flat 1D array.</div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 5: Histogram & Binning
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">5. Histogram & Binning</h2>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Histograms and binning</strong> convert continuous data into discrete categories. This is fundamental for visualizing distributions, feature engineering (turning continuous features into categorical ones), and understanding data density. NumPy provides the computational backend — Plotly and Matplotlib add the visualization.</p>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Gaussian (Normal) probability density function — the most important continuous distribution in statistics:</strong></p>'
+ '<div class="katex-block">$$f(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{(x-\\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right)$$</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">f(x)</div><div class="card-body">Probability density at value x — height of the bell curve. Note: this is density, not probability; only integrals over intervals give probabilities.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">μ</div><div class="card-body">Mean — center of the bell curve. Shifts the distribution left/right.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">σ</div><div class="card-body">Standard deviation — controls the width. Larger σ = wider, flatter curve. Smaller σ = narrower, taller spike.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">1/(σ√2π)</div><div class="card-body">Normalization constant — ensures total area under the curve equals 1, making f a valid probability density.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">exp(-(x-μ)²/2σ²)</div><div class="card-body">The exponential decay term. Squared deviation in the numerator gives the symmetric bell shape; division by 2σ² controls how fast it decays.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Z-score — how many standard deviations a value is from the mean:</strong></p>'
+ '<div class="katex-block">$$z = \\frac{x - \\mu}{\\sigma}$$</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">z</div><div class="card-body">Z-score — standardized value. z = 0 means x is at the mean; z = 2 means x is two std deviations above the mean.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">x</div><div class="card-body">The raw observation we want to standardize.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">|z| > 3</div><div class="card-body">Common outlier rule. Under a normal distribution, only 0.27% of data lies beyond ±3σ.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Use in ML</div><div class="card-body">Z-score normalization (StandardScaler) makes features unit-variance, mean-zero — essential for gradient-based models.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">np.histogram</div><div class="card-body">Computes bin counts and bin edges. Does NOT plot — returns (counts, edges) tuple. Use for computation, not visualization.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">np.histogram2d</div><div class="card-body">2D histogram for joint distribution of two variables. Returns a 2D count matrix — perfect for heatmaps.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">np.digitize</div><div class="card-body">Assigns each value to a bin index. Essential for converting continuous features to categorical (e.g., age groups).</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">np.bincount</div><div class="card-body">Counts occurrences of each non-negative integer. Faster than np.unique for integer arrays. Perfect for label counting.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\n<span class="cm"># np.histogram — compute (not plot!) a histogram</span>\ndata = np.random.<span class="fn">normal</span>(<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">1000</span>)\ncounts, bin_edges = np.<span class="fn">histogram</span>(data, bins=<span class="num">20</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Counts:"</span>, counts)          <span class="cm"># array of 20 counts</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Edges: "</span>, bin_edges.shape)   <span class="cm"># (21,) — N+1 edges for N bins</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Custom bin edges</span>\nages = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">5</span>, <span class="num">12</span>, <span class="num">18</span>, <span class="num">25</span>, <span class="num">35</span>, <span class="num">45</span>, <span class="num">60</span>, <span class="num">72</span>, <span class="num">15</span>, <span class="num">29</span>])\nbins = [<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">13</span>, <span class="num">18</span>, <span class="num">65</span>, <span class="num">100</span>]   <span class="cm"># child, teen, adult, senior</span>\ncounts, _ = np.<span class="fn">histogram</span>(ages, bins=bins)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Age groups:"</span>, counts)       <span class="cm"># [2 2 5 1]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># np.digitize — assign bin indices</span>\nbin_indices = np.<span class="fn">digitize</span>(ages, bins=[<span class="num">13</span>, <span class="num">18</span>, <span class="num">65</span>])\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Bin indices:"</span>, bin_indices)  <span class="cm"># each age \u2192 0, 1, 2, or 3</span>\n<span class="cm"># 0=child(<13), 1=teen(13-18), 2=adult(18-65), 3=senior(65+)</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># np.bincount — fast integer counting</span>\nlabels = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">0</span>])\nclass_counts = np.<span class="fn">bincount</span>(labels)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Class counts:"</span>, class_counts)  <span class="cm"># [3 3 3]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Weighted bincount (for computing class means)</span>\nvalues = np.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="num">10</span>, <span class="num">20</span>, <span class="num">30</span>, <span class="num">25</span>, <span class="num">15</span>, <span class="num">35</span>, <span class="num">28</span>, <span class="num">22</span>, <span class="num">12</span>])\nclass_sums = np.<span class="fn">bincount</span>(labels, weights=values)\nclass_means = class_sums / class_counts\n<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Class means:"</span>, class_means)  <span class="cm"># [12.33 22.33 31.0]</span></code></pre></div>'

/* --- Plotly: 2D Histogram Heatmap (EN) --- */
+ '<div id="plot-hist2d-en" class="plotly-graph"></div>'
